\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Curl and the Hodge star}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{The undergraduate quotation keeps #{\operatorname{curl}}, and the
forms paragraph keeps the Hodge star #{\star} of its source. Hestenes's
geometric algebra (\citek{hestenes2012clifford}) writes duality as a
product with the pseudoscalar, so it joins the other products of the
algebra: with the convention #{A\wedge\star B=\langle A,B\rangle\psI},
the Hodge dual of a #{k}-vector #{A} is #{\star A=\tilde A\psI}, where
#{\tilde A} reverses the order of the vector factors of #{A}. Since
#{\psI^2=-1} in spacetime, this is #{F\psI^{-1}} on the bivector #{F}
and #{-J\psI^{-1}} on the vector #{J}. The geometric paragraph writes
the exterior part #{\covwedge F}. The pseudoscalar #{\psI} stays
distinct from the identity map #{\bbI}.}

\p{Scott O. Wilson defines a cochain star on a closed orientable
manifold by pairing his cup product with the fundamental class,
for a positive-definite inner product orthogonal across degrees
\citet{Definition 6.1, p. 17}{scottowilson2005cochains}.
He states that this star need not be invertible, and that its
square need not equal #{\pm\bbI}
\citet{p. 18}{scottowilson2005cochains}.
Desbrun, Hirani, Leok and Marsden send a primal cochain to a
dual cochain by the ratio of the dual volume to the primal
volume
\citet{Definition 6.1, p. 15}{desbrunhiranileokmarsden2005dec}.
They write an asterisk for that operator, and their star names
the dual cell. Neither operator is the Hodge dual
#{\star A=\tilde A\psI} of the first paragraph.}
